{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1786504422,"nonce":"nRfzx3Ntt4Dv12W4BNQn5jphqfI=","target":"d9756d1f7615118f28cc570bafe08c73c1864cfc40e087787fdc356de94d1c9e","message":"**What is wrong.** `MemberStates` and `Service.OccurrenceStates` in `internal/alerts/service/seam.go:112-160` are an exported type and an exported method built for a named consumer that never called them. `grep -rn \"OccurrenceStates\\|MemberStates\"` across the whole repository returns eight matches and all eight are inside `seam.go` itself — the declaration, the three `MemberStates{}` returns and the signature. Zero callers in production, zero in tests.\n\n**The demonstration.** The doc-comment names the consumer and the use case precisely: *\"It exists so that `grouping` can roll up `firing_count`, `acked_count` and the group's own state without reading `alert_occurrences` itself.\"* `internal/grouping` does not reference it. The type is one of the seam's declared reasons to exist, in a file whose header describes itself as *\"the seam `internal/grouping` calls across\"*.\n\nIt also could not serve that purpose at the size it was designed for. `OccurrenceStates` takes `occurrenceIDs []uuid.UUID` and loops, issuing two queries per element — `s.occurrences.GetByID` at `:138` and `s.alerts.GetByID` at `:148` — so a group with 100 members costs 200 round trips. The method whose stated job is a batch roll-up is the one shape a batch roll-up must not have. The API it would feed is documented elsewhere in the same file as fanning out over every open member (`AcknowledgeAs`, `:170-180`), and storm-mode groups are explicitly expected to be large.\n\n`tools/lintreach` was written for exactly this defect class — its own header calls it *\"code that compiles, lints, type-checks, is exported, is schema-validated and is sometimes rendered in a UI form — and is wired to nothing\"* — and it cannot see this one. Its three checks are `nowrite`/`noread` on struct fields, `spec-only`/`go-only` on `json:` tags against `api/openapi/openapi.yaml`, and `noctor`, which is scoped to *\"an exported New\\* constructor or Compute\\* identity function with no caller outside its own file\"* (`tools/lintreach/main.go:24-47`). An unreferenced exported method with neither prefix falls between all three. `MemberStates`'s three fields are written and read inside `OccurrenceStates`, so `nowrite`/`noread` see a live struct.\n\n**Why it matters.** Two costs, and the second is the larger. The immediate one is that a reader of `seam.go` believes `grouping` rolls up member state through this method, which is a false picture of how the alerts/grouping boundary actually works. The lasting one is that the gate built to catch unwired features has a hole shaped like this, so the next unreferenced exported method is equally invisible — and the header of `lintreach` records that fifteen-plus instances of this class were found by running the product rather than by reading it.\n\n**Where.** `internal/alerts/service/seam.go:112-126` (`MemberStates`), `:128-160` (`OccurrenceStates`, with the N+1 at `:137-158`), `tools/lintreach/main.go:24-47` (the three checks, none of which cover this).\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** Either `MemberStates` and `OccurrenceStates` are deleted, or a `grouping` caller exists and the method resolves state, ack and raw severity for N occurrences in one query. Separately, `lintreach` grows a check for exported methods with no caller outside their own package, so this class fails CI rather than an audit.","files":null}]}